-- title:
--   Off both axes with irrational angle: the genuine residual
-- statement:
--   **Diaz's conjecture off both axes, with argument angle not a rational multiple of $\pi$.**
--
--   If $u \neq 0$ has $\mathrm{Re}\,u \neq 0$ and $\mathrm{Im}\,u \notin \pi\mathbb{Q}$,
--   with $|u|$ algebraic and $e^{u}$ not real, then $e^{u}$ is transcendental.
--
--   **Why this node exists: the parent leaf is not a fragment of the conjecture, it is the
--   conjecture.** Candidacy is invariant under scaling by non-zero rationals, and rational
--   scaling moves $\mathrm{Im}\,u$ *across* $\pi\mathbb{Z}$. An off-axes $u$ with
--   $\mathrm{Im}\,u = q\pi$ scales by $\mathrm{den}(q)$ onto a point of the sibling leaf
--   `DiazModulus.diaz_of_exp_real_generic`; a point of that leaf divides by $|n|+1$ back into
--   the off-axes rational-angle region. So the rational-angle half of the parent *is* the
--   sibling leaf, and the parent — carrying one hypothesis fewer — implies it, and implies
--   the conjecture outright given Hermite–Lindemann and transcendence of $\pi$, both of which
--   this mission owns.
--
--   That leaves this node as the genuine residual: the region where no rational scaling
--   relates a counterexample to the real branch.
--
--   **The dividing line.** Off the imaginary axis,
--   $$\mathrm{Im}\,u \in \pi\mathbb{Q}
--   \iff (u, \bar u, 2\pi i) \text{ is } \mathbb{Q}\text{-dependent}
--   \iff e^{u}/|e^{u}| \text{ is a root of unity}.$$
--
--   **Smallest concrete open case.** Is $|\mathrm{Log}(2+i)|$ algebraic — equivalently, is
--   $\tfrac14(\log 5)^{2} + \arctan(\tfrac12)^{2}$ the square of an algebraic number, or
--   $\mathrm{Log}(2+i)\,\mathrm{Log}(2-i) \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$? Note $1+i$ does
--   **not** belong here: its argument is $\pi/4$, so scaling by $4$ sends it to
--   $\mathrm{Log}(-4)$, giving $(\log 4)^{2} + \pi^{2}$, a point of the sibling leaf.
--
--   **Why the mission's tools stop.** The free-ring no-go recorded for this mission is
--   *exact* here rather than a cut-down: the sub-$\tilde{\mathcal L}$ generated by everything
--   known about a candidate has exactly one extra generator, $2\pi i$. Strong six
--   exponentials, ordinary six, and ordinary four are all provably unable to fire, and
--   $2\pi i$ opens no new $2\times3$ template. Every sufficient input known — the strong four
--   exponentials conjecture, Schanuel at $n = 2$, algebraic independence of logarithms —
--   implies the whole conjecture rather than this node.
--
--   What is needed first is a theorem sensitive to $2\pi i$ as a *distinguished* element
--   rather than an anonymous basis vector of $\mathcal{L}$. The five exponentials theorem has
--   the right distinguished-quotient shape but still requires a $2\times2$
--   $\mathcal{L}$-template that the no-go forbids.
--
--   **Prior art.** That the difficulty lies off both axes — on effectively complex $u$, not
--   real and not purely imaginary — was already recorded in the mission author's own notes
--   before this mission existed. The contribution here is the formal reduction and the
--   identification of the rational-angle line, not that observation.
--
--   No novelty is claimed for the scaling argument, which is elementary.
--
